I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and reusable refinement utilities, then build a chemically plausible phase inventory before fitting. I’ll keep the refinement residual-led and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and final GSAS-II project all agree.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type NiWO₄, with unreacted WO₃ and NiO as realistic residuals at 700 °C. I’m now testing those against the peak positions and searching the COD inventory for alternate records/polymorphs, so no phase is accepted from chemistry alone.

Peak-family matching now supports three crystalline phases: monoclinic NiWO₄, room-temperature monoclinic WO₃, and rocksalt NiO. Each has several phase-specific reflections (not just one coincident line), so I’m carrying all three into a common-profile GSAS-II refinement and will re-challenge omissions/alternate WO₃ records after the baseline converges.

The WO₃ polymorph screen rejects the tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, and alternate small-cell monoclinic records by missing/split peak families. Two equivalent large-cell monoclinic descriptions remain plausible; I’ll compare their physical structural models under identical nuisance parameters while refining the exact NiWO₄ and NiO models.

The first common-profile fit reaches Rwp ≈ 6.28%, but it exposed two baseline defects I’m correcting before judging phases: the sample and phase scales were redundantly free, and free U went negative. I’m fixing the sample scale, enforcing U = 0 with nonnegative W/X, and pruning SH/L because it gave negligible improvement and was 97% correlated with zero.

The common-profile widths are adequate across low, middle, and high angle, but the residual has a coherent cross-phase intensity trend: low-angle peaks are generally overcalculated while many high-angle families are undercalculated. I’m testing polarization and Bragg–Brentano surface roughness before any phase texture, as this trend is not phase-specific.

Refining one global polarization term removes the cross-phase trend (Rwp 6.23% → 3.73%; refined effective polarization 0.844). Surface roughness is not retained: combined with polarization it becomes negative and highly correlated while improving Rwp by only 0.003%. I’m now testing size and strain independently for each phase, accepting only replay-stable, non-bound phase-specific broadening.

Only NiWO₄ shows supported phase-specific broadening. Isotropic microstrain improves Rwp to 3.698%, and a [0 1 1] uniaxial model improves it further to 3.612%; both components are well determined and no covariance warning appears. WO₃/NiO size or strain terms either hit the neutral bound, worsened the fit, or became negative, so they are pruned. Next I’m challenging preferred orientation axis-by-axis under this accepted profile.

The exact NiWO₄ record was structurally too coarse, not chemically wrong. Refining its symmetry-allowed coordinates produces a large, reproducible improvement (Rwp 3.612% → 2.184%); starting from the ZnWO₄ analogue converges to the same NiWO₄ coordinates. I’m checking bond geometry, coordinate uncertainties, and replay stability before retaining those coordinates.

The three-phase inventory is now coherent: NiWO₄, residual WO₃, and NiO. I’m finishing the residual-led checks—geometry, replay-stable broadening/texture choices, and exact GSAS-II/export agreement—before writing the final files.

The refined NiWO₄ geometry passes a physical screen: Ni–O is 2.028–2.106 Å and W–O is 1.800–2.104 Å, both six-coordinate. The earlier anisotropic strain term moved substantially after the atomic model improved, so I’m re-testing it from neutral values rather than carrying it forward automatically.

The replay comparison supports a small isotropic NiWO₄ microstrain (90 ± 14 µstrain). The best anisotropic split improves Rwp by only 0.00047 percentage point and its axial/equatorial difference is under 2σ, so that extra term is being pruned. I’m now re-challenging preferred orientation against this cleaner common profile.

All three phases survive an omission/refit challenge by a wide margin: removing NiWO₄, WO₃, or NiO raises Rwp from 2.184% to 33.47%, 22.74%, or 6.53%, respectively. NiO is therefore not just a scale/background surrogate despite being the smallest phase. Texture remains a marginal, axis-sensitive correction and is being left neutral.

Revisiting the WO₃ source after the NiWO₄ structure was corrected changed the selection materially: the monoclinic P2₁/c record now gives Rwp 1.857%, versus 2.184% for triclinic P-1 and 3.982% for the other monoclinic record under the same nuisance model. I’m switching to P2₁/c and rebuilding the profile/watch-list tests around it, as required when the inventory source changes.

With the selected monoclinic WO₃ record, phase-specific widths are genuinely separable: combined isotropic strain terms lower Rwp to 1.771% with positive W/X and only moderate correlations. I’m checking whether crystallite size or anisotropy adds replay-stable information before fixing the final profile.

The rebuilt profile is now replay-stable and physically bounded. It supports NiWO₄ size broadening (1.17 µm) plus family-dependent strain for all three phases; alternate axes either fit worse or diverge, while the selected axes remain finite and significant. I’m doing the final texture re-challenge on this accepted profile, then I’ll freeze/export it.

Texture is pruned: WO₃ effects are ≤2.3σ, NiO texture is unidentifiable, and the nominal 4σ NiWO₄ term improves some isolated families while worsening several others, so it fails the coherence test. The no-texture model remains the final candidate; phase omission under that exact profile again strongly rejects dropping any of the three phases.

The final model has been replayed four times after pruning unsupported terms and exported directly from that exact `final.gpx`. I’m running one last cross-file audit now: project fractions/statistics, CIF cells and chemistry, and point-by-point agreement of `fit.csv` with the saved GSAS-II arrays.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- NiWO₄, P2/c: 60.301(36) wt%
- WO₃, P2₁/c: 29.470(27) wt%
- NiO, Fm-3m: 10.229(38) wt%

Final fit: Rwp = 1.744%, Rp = 1.372%, GOF = 1.208. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases; amorphous content is not quantified.

